<article class="env-lemma" id="01MD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01MD">Lemma <span data-tag="01MD">27.8.9</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a graded ring. The scheme $\text{Proj}(S)$ is quasi-compact if and only if there exist finitely many homogeneous elements $f_1, \ldots , f_ n \in S_{+}$ such that $S_{+} \subset \sqrt{(f_1, \ldots , f_ n)}$. In this case $\text{Proj}(S) = D_+(f_1) \cup \ldots \cup D_+(f_ n)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Given such a collection of elements the standard affine opens $D_{+}(f_ i)$ cover $\text{Proj}(S)$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00JP" data-tag="00JP">10.57.3</a>. Conversely, if $\text{Proj}(S)$ is quasi-compact, then we may cover it by finitely many standard opens $D_{+}(f_ i)$, $i = 1, \ldots , n$ and we see that $S_{+} \subset \sqrt{(f_1, \ldots , f_ n)}$ by the lemma referenced above. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>